From 6c31ede4320763293672f1edcfa501ce42ec5720 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fountain5405 <Fountain5405@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 10 Nov 2026 15:06:35 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 8/9] generalized-bulletproofs: shrink each vector commitment's
 weight vector to the length it uses

For each vector commitment, prove() builds cg_weights with ScalarVector::new(n),
a vector of the full row count, then truncates it to the highest index any
constraint uses. Truncation keeps the capacity. In an FCMP++ proof most
commitments use a few hundred of n rows, so each one held about 1 MB it never
used. At 128 inputs there are about 270 of them, about 283 MB in all.

shrink_to_fit() now releases the rest.
- Measured (128-input wallet build on a 12-core Ryzen 9 3900X (16 GB RAM, SATA SSD), 3 rounds, with the previous
  commit): peak memory 1,262.9 MB becomes 1,001.9 MB. That is 286 MB less
  than monerodato.3's 1,287.8 MB.
- At 32 inputs the peak drops by 18.6 MB; at 8 inputs it is unchanged.
- The weights are public (they come from the constraints and the challenge
  z), so nothing secret is involved. The lengths are public too.

The proofs are unchanged (prove_output_unchanged).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 crypto/generalized-bulletproofs/src/arithmetic_circuit_proof.rs | 2 ++
 1 file changed, 2 insertions(+)

diff --git a/crypto/generalized-bulletproofs/src/arithmetic_circuit_proof.rs b/crypto/generalized-bulletproofs/src/arithmetic_circuit_proof.rs
index 3bc7c5f16..bda44089d 100644
--- a/crypto/generalized-bulletproofs/src/arithmetic_circuit_proof.rs
+++ b/crypto/generalized-bulletproofs/src/arithmetic_circuit_proof.rs
@@ -579,6 +579,8 @@ where
           }
         }
         cg.0.truncate(cg_hi + 1);
+        // `new(n)` allocated the full length; keep only what is used
+        cg.0.shrink_to_fit();
         cg
       };
 
